Assess the Health of an OpenShift Cluster (Simplified Guide)
Cluster health is checked by inspecting:
1️⃣ Operators
2️⃣ Pods and namespaces
3️⃣ Resource usage (CPU/Memory)
4️⃣ Cluster events and alerts
5️⃣ Node status
6️⃣ Logs and debugging tools
________________________________________
1️⃣ Check Cluster Operators
Operators manage and maintain cluster services automatically.
Examples of operators:
•	authentication
•	console
•	dns
•	kube-apiserver
•	ingress
•	monitoring
These are core OpenShift components.
List cluster operators
oc get clusteroperators
Example output:
NAME            VERSION   AVAILABLE   PROGRESSING   DEGRADED
authentication  4.18.6    True        False         False
console         4.18.6    True        False         False
dns             4.18.6    True        False         False
Meaning
Column	Meaning
AVAILABLE	Operator is working
PROGRESSING	Operator is updating
DEGRADED	Operator has problems
Healthy operator state:
AVAILABLE = True
PROGRESSING = False
DEGRADED = False
________________________________________
2️⃣ Inspect an Operator
Example: DNS operator
oc describe clusteroperator dns
For structured output:
oc get clusteroperator dns -o yaml
Example YAML part:
status:
  conditions:
  - type: Available
    status: True
    message: DNS "default" is available
________________________________________
3️⃣ Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM)
OLM manages optional add-on operators.
Example:
•	storage operator
•	MetalLB operator
List installed operators
oc get operators
Example:
NAME
lvms-operator.openshift-storage
metallb-operator.metallb-system
________________________________________
4️⃣ Find Pods Used by Operators
Operators run pods inside namespaces.
Example:
oc get pods -n openshift-dns-operator
Output:
dns-operator-74bb989655-vgm7t
________________________________________
5️⃣ Inspect Pod Status
oc get pod dns-operator-74bb989655-vgm7t -o json | jq .status
Shows:
•	readiness
•	container status
•	health conditions
Example condition:
type: Ready
status: True
Meaning: Pod is healthy.
________________________________________
6️⃣ Show Pod Labels
Labels help identify resources.
oc get pods -n openshift-etcd --show-labels
Example:
NAME           STATUS   LABELS
etcd-master01  Running  app=etcd,k8s-app=etcd
________________________________________
7️⃣ Check Cluster Resource Usage
Use:
oc adm top pods
For all namespaces:
oc adm top pods -A --sum
Example:
CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)
3121m       9123Mi
________________________________________
8️⃣ Check Container Usage
Example:
oc adm top pods etcd-master01 -n openshift-etcd --containers
Output:
NAME           CPU     MEMORY
etcd           72m     334Mi
etcd-metrics   8m      31Mi
This helps detect:
•	memory leaks
•	CPU spikes
•	overloaded containers
________________________________________
9️⃣ View Cluster Metrics (Web Console)
OpenShift Web Console:
Home → Overview
Shows:
•	CPU usage
•	Memory usage
•	Storage usage
•	Top resource consumers
________________________________________
🔟 View Project Metrics
Project page:
Workloads → Pods
Shows:
•	CPU
•	memory
•	storage usage
Useful for troubleshooting application problems.
________________________________________
11️⃣ Query Prometheus Metrics
Go to:
Observe → Metrics
Run Prometheus queries to monitor cluster.
Example metrics:
•	CPU usage
•	memory usage
•	network traffic
________________________________________
12️⃣ Check Cluster Events
Events show important actions in the cluster.
Example:
oc get events
All namespaces:
oc get events -A
Sort by time:
oc get events --sort-by .metadata.creationTimestamp
Example output:
Normal   Scheduled   Pod scheduled to node
Warning  Failed      Container failed to start
________________________________________
13️⃣ View Monitoring Stack
Monitoring runs in namespace:
openshift-monitoring
Check monitoring components:
oc get all -n openshift-monitoring
Example:
prometheus-k8s-0
alertmanager-main-0
cluster-monitoring-operator
________________________________________
14️⃣ Query Prometheus Alerts
Example command:
oc -n openshift-monitoring exec -c prometheus prometheus-k8s-0 \
-- curl -s http://localhost:9090/api/v1/alerts | jq
This retrieves:
•	active alerts
•	monitoring warnings
________________________________________
15️⃣ Query Alertmanager Alerts
oc -n openshift-monitoring exec -c alertmanager alertmanager-main-0 \
-- curl -s http://localhost:9093/api/v2/alerts | jq
Alertmanager sends alerts to:
•	email
•	Slack
•	PagerDuty
________________________________________
16️⃣ Check Node Status
List cluster nodes:
oc get nodes
Example:
NAME       STATUS   ROLES
master01   Ready    control-plane
Status meanings
Status	Meaning
Ready	Node healthy
NotReady	Node problem
________________________________________
17️⃣ Inspect Node Details
oc describe node master01
Shows:
•	conditions
•	capacity
•	resources
•	kubelet status
________________________________________
18️⃣ Check Node Resources
Example:
oc get node master01 -o jsonpath='
{.status.allocatable}
'
Example:
cpu: 5500m
memory: 15225452Ki
pods: 250
________________________________________
19️⃣ Check Node Health Conditions
Example command:
oc get node master01 -o json | jq '.status.conditions'
Possible conditions:
Condition	Meaning
MemoryPressure	Low memory
DiskPressure	Disk almost full
PIDPressure	Too many processes
Ready	Node operational
Example healthy state:
MemoryPressure = False
DiskPressure = False
PIDPressure = False
Ready = True
________________________________________
20️⃣ View Node Logs
Cluster admins can view logs:
oc adm node-logs master01
Filter examples:
kubelet logs
oc adm node-logs master01 -u kubelet
CRI-O logs
oc adm node-logs master01 -u crio
last log line
oc adm node-logs master01 -u crio --tail 1
________________________________________
21️⃣ Debug a Node
Start debug session:
oc debug node/master01
Enter host filesystem:
chroot /host
Check services:
systemctl is-active kubelet
systemctl is-active crio
Healthy result:
active
________________________________________
22️⃣ View Pod Logs
oc logs pod-name
Example:
oc logs myapp-54fcdcd9d7
For multi-container pods:
oc logs pod-name -c container-name
________________________________________
23️⃣ Debug a Pod
If pod fails:
oc debug pod/myapp
Debug command:
•	creates temporary pod
•	starts shell
•	disables probes
________________________________________
24️⃣ Debug as Root or Non-root
Run as non-root:
oc debug job/test --as-user=1000000
Run as root:
oc debug pod/myapp --as-root
________________________________________
25️⃣ Collect Support Data
For Red Hat support cases.
Run:
oc adm must-gather
Output directory:
must-gather.local/
Compress it:
tar cvaf mustgather.tar must-gather/
Send to Red Hat support.
________________________________________
26️⃣ Inspect Specific Resources
Example:
oc adm inspect clusteroperator/kube-apiserver
Limit logs:
oc adm inspect clusteroperator/kube-apiserver --since 10m
